28. FINAL CONTROLLED METHODS STATEMENT
======================================================================

The study uses patient-specific ventricular geometries derived from cardiac
MRI segmentation, converted into three-dimensional tetrahedral finite-element
domains and analyzed using an anisotropic Holzapfel–Ogden-type hyperelastic
solid-mechanics formulation with a nearly incompressible treatment.
Myocardial fiber orientation is represented using a continuous transmural
field ranging from +60 degrees at the endocardium to -60 degrees at the
epicardium. Gravity is varied using predefined multipliers of 1.00, 0.75,
0.50, 0.38, 0.16, and 0.00 relative to standard terrestrial gravity. The
primary outputs are peak von Mises stress, peak principal strain, maximum
ventricular displacement, LV cavity volume, myocardial volume, and
ventricular strain energy. Numerical verification comprises geometry,
mesh-independence, nonlinear solver convergence, residual assessment,
failed-element assessment, boundary-condition consistency, and energy-based
checks, with sensitivity analysis used to evaluate robustness where
execution evidence is available.

The exact numerical implementation of all model parameters and solver
settings must be traceable to the executed computational model before final
manuscript freeze.

